KATHERINE GERTON: No, that's OK.

00:15:18.830 --> 00:15:22.910
Just mentioning, we
use the Posit products

00:15:22.910 --> 00:15:27.320
and get back deployment from
our version control repository.

00:15:27.320 --> 00:15:29.360
My team kind of has
a workflow where

00:15:29.360 --> 00:15:32.450
we do like a dev deployment.

00:15:32.450 --> 00:15:35.610
So we have our master deployment
and that kind of sits there.

00:15:35.610 --> 00:15:37.760
And we also have a
get-back deployment

00:15:37.760 --> 00:15:38.940
from our develop branch.

00:15:38.940 --> 00:15:42.170
So any changes that we make can
be tested there, vetted out,

00:15:42.170 --> 00:15:44.360
before it goes to the
version of the app

00:15:44.360 --> 00:15:47.370
that our customers are using.

00:15:47.370 --> 00:15:49.310
And so, that can
kind of troubleshoot

00:15:49.310 --> 00:15:52.670
some of those issues
where it's like, "Oh I

00:15:52.670 --> 00:15:55.010
forgot I installed the
dev version of a package

00:15:55.010 --> 00:15:58.070
on my local thing trying
to get this new feature,"

00:15:58.070 --> 00:16:01.040
but you didn't fix your
manifest before it goes

00:16:01.040 --> 00:16:02.990
to the deployment, and
so that would break,

00:16:02.990 --> 00:16:05.150
and you can find that out
from your dev deployments

00:16:05.150 --> 00:16:08.240
before it ever gets to
that customer interaction

00:16:08.240 --> 00:16:09.050
interruption.

00:16:09.050 --> 00:16:10.100
Yeah.

