with Workbench so far?

00:25:03.320 --> 00:25:04.950
I didn't know it
did all that stuff.

00:25:04.950 --> 00:25:07.215
And in particular, I'm
very curious about Copilot

00:25:07.215 --> 00:25:09.590
in VS Code I know that's not
going to be something that's

00:25:09.590 --> 00:25:13.490
in the RStudio-like products
natively, for good reasons.

00:25:13.490 --> 00:25:15.680
But is that something
you all are doing

00:25:15.680 --> 00:25:18.196
Workbench, so far so good?

00:25:18.196 --> 00:25:20.790
Well I don't know about
all the Copilot stuff,

00:25:20.790 --> 00:25:23.160
so maybe someone from
Posit can talk to that.

00:25:23.160 --> 00:25:29.850
But yeah, that was kind of how I
sold Posit products internally,

00:25:29.850 --> 00:25:31.140
because I think--

00:25:31.140 --> 00:25:35.130
You know, I'm such a fan of
the new brand rename to Posit,

00:25:35.130 --> 00:25:37.200
because I think
previously it was really

00:25:37.200 --> 00:25:42.840
hard to make a case for an R and
Python approach to data science

00:25:42.840 --> 00:25:46.410
with "RStudio," formerly
known as RStudio products,

00:25:46.410 --> 00:25:49.200
because everyone assumed that
that was all you could do.

00:25:49.200 --> 00:25:51.060
But like I mentioned
earlier, when

00:25:51.060 --> 00:25:53.310
I was kind of going around
trying to make the business

00:25:53.310 --> 00:25:58.270
case for this tooling,
I was like, OK,

00:25:58.270 --> 00:26:03.360
I know that a lot of our Python
users use VS Code and Python,

00:26:03.360 --> 00:26:06.540
so I need to show them
what a piece of their work

00:26:06.540 --> 00:26:10.110
would look like in Python
and VS Code on this platform

00:26:10.110 --> 00:26:12.780
to kind of fully convince
them that it's not

00:26:12.780 --> 00:26:16.620
just theoretical, that yes you
can spin up a VS Code instance.

00:26:16.620 --> 00:26:18.300
But like, show
them, yeah, here's

00:26:18.300 --> 00:26:20.730
me, little old me,
that doesn't ever

00:26:20.730 --> 00:26:23.700
work in VS Code or Python--

00:26:23.700 --> 00:26:26.310
not ever but rarely--

00:26:26.310 --> 00:26:30.120
spinning up a machine
learning ops kind

00:26:30.120 --> 00:26:32.950
of system in those products.

00:26:32.950 --> 00:26:35.940
So yeah, we're
definitely using that.

00:26:35.940 --> 00:26:38.760
We haven't really
deployed Workbench

00:26:38.760 --> 00:26:41.940
out to the broader
team yet, because again

00:26:41.940 --> 00:26:44.280
taking that approach of
like, iron out the kinks

00:26:44.280 --> 00:26:48.630
first before you open it up
to a broader group of people.

00:26:48.630 --> 00:26:50.370
So myself and one
of my colleagues

00:26:50.370 --> 00:26:52.770
are kind of working
with our DataOps team

00:26:52.770 --> 00:26:56.790
to make sure that it's a really
good experience once we do open

00:26:56.790 --> 00:27:00.090
the gates for the rest
of our organization,

00:27:00.090 --> 00:27:02.400
and making sure that
our Python users are

00:27:02.400 --> 00:27:04.270
able to use their
tools of choice

00:27:04.270 --> 00:27:06.245
as well is a big part of that.

00:27:06.245 --> 00:27:07.000
Awesome.

00:27:07.000 --> 00:27:08.410
Thanks.

00:27:08.410 --> 00:27:10.480
RACHEL DEMPSEY: Thank you.

00:27:10.480 --> 00:27:12.985
So something that I
wanted to ask you about

00:27:12.985 --> 00:27:15.550
is, I know leading
up to this, we

00:27:15.550 --> 00:27:17.800
were talking a little bit
about developing communities

00:27:17.800 --> 00:27:18.790
of belonging.

00:27:18.790 --> 00:27:20.830
And one of the communities
you're a part of

00:27:20.830 --> 00:27:21.985
is Data Science by Design.

00:27:21.985 --> 00:27:24.610
And I just want to make sure we
chatted a little bit about that

00:27:24.610 --> 00:27:25.455
too.

00:27:25.455 --> 00:27:26.740
Oh my god, yeah.

00:27:26.740 --> 00:27:29.870
I could talk forever
about this community.

00:27:29.870 --> 00:27:34.720
So I hesitate-- I'm
technically on the leadership

00:27:34.720 --> 00:27:37.810
committee for Data
Science by Design,

00:27:37.810 --> 00:27:40.570
which was like, such an
honor to be a part of that.

00:27:40.570 --> 00:27:46.120
I've been doing that since
their first meeting last,

00:27:46.120 --> 00:27:47.020
what was that?

00:27:47.020 --> 00:27:50.320
Summer of 2021?

00:27:50.320 --> 00:27:52.390
The pandemic years all
blur together for me.

00:27:52.390 --> 00:27:59.890
But yeah, like this community
is just like, small but mighty.

00:27:59.890 --> 00:28:01.030
We have a lot of--

00:28:01.030 --> 00:28:04.150
So I come from kind of
an organizing background

00:28:04.150 --> 00:28:08.140
as well, so doing a lot
of community organizing.

00:28:08.140 --> 00:28:10.810
And we have a lot of people in
the community organizing world

00:28:10.810 --> 00:28:11.410
there.

00:28:11.410 --> 00:28:14.560
And a lot of us are big fans
of Adrienne Marie Brown,

00:28:14.560 --> 00:28:19.060
and we've kind of talked about--
who wrote a book that for me

00:28:19.060 --> 00:28:22.510
was like, life-changing,
called Emergent Strategy--

00:28:22.510 --> 00:28:25.630
and we really have taken the
approach with this community

00:28:25.630 --> 00:28:31.840
to focus on, she calls
it in Emergent Strategy,

00:28:31.840 --> 00:28:35.050
an "inch-wide, mile-deep"
organizing strategy,

00:28:35.050 --> 00:28:38.410
something along those lines,
which is essentially just like

00:28:38.410 --> 00:28:43.060
prioritizing quality and deep
relationships and a vibrant

00:28:43.060 --> 00:28:47.390
community over the breadth and
the largeness of the community.

00:28:47.390 --> 00:28:50.290
So, I think that approach
has been amazing.

00:28:50.290 --> 00:28:53.260
So yeah, it's such
a great group.

00:28:53.260 --> 00:28:56.777
We do a lot of like,
creative coding stuff.

00:28:56.777 --> 00:28:58.360
A lot of the people
in our community--

00:28:58.360 --> 00:29:01.570
actually Ijamaka
and Charlotte are

00:29:01.570 --> 00:29:05.380
going to be doing a
workshop on, at Posit,

00:29:05.380 --> 00:29:08.230
like "Bringing Out
Your Inner Artist,"

00:29:08.230 --> 00:29:09.410
or something like that.

00:29:09.410 --> 00:29:11.320
So, we've got a
lot of people that

00:29:11.320 --> 00:29:15.430
are really interested in the
intersections between art,

00:29:15.430 --> 00:29:18.850
design, data science,
community organizing.

00:29:18.850 --> 00:29:22.990
Like, it's quite an
interesting mix of people,

00:29:22.990 --> 00:29:25.460
but it all kind of
ties together somehow.

00:29:25.460 --> 00:29:27.820
I could not recommend
that group more.

00:29:27.820 --> 00:29:31.450
We actually have an anthology
coming out pretty soon here.

00:29:31.450 --> 00:29:37.930
We released an anthology
last year that was,

00:29:37.930 --> 00:29:40.242
what was the title of it?

00:29:40.242 --> 00:29:41.950
Now I'm forgetting
because I'm so focused

00:29:41.950 --> 00:29:42.940
on this new anthology.

00:29:42.940 --> 00:29:45.740
But the new anthology
is "Our Environment."

00:29:45.740 --> 00:29:49.810
So we're really talking about
environments, broadly defined.

00:29:49.810 --> 00:29:52.330
A lot of environmental
data scientists

00:29:52.330 --> 00:29:54.640
submitted work
there, but we also

00:29:54.640 --> 00:29:57.460
have topics on things
like community-building,

00:29:57.460 --> 00:30:00.050
your local environment,
things like that.

00:30:00.050 --> 00:30:02.950
So yeah, I don't
even know quite where

00:30:02.950 --> 00:30:04.310
to start with that community.

00:30:04.310 --> 00:30:08.170
But again where when you
find a group of people

00:30:08.170 --> 00:30:09.590
that's just so supportive--

00:30:09.590 --> 00:30:11.920
I think entering
the data science

00:30:11.920 --> 00:30:14.830
field can be so overwhelming,
because there are so

00:30:14.830 --> 00:30:18.130
many facets to it, and
things that you feel like you

00:30:18.130 --> 00:30:20.290
need to be an expert in.

00:30:20.290 --> 00:30:21.130
And you don't.

00:30:21.130 --> 00:30:23.420
Like, everyone has
their own expertise.

00:30:23.420 --> 00:30:25.060
Everyone has their
own lived experience

00:30:25.060 --> 00:30:26.890
that they bring
to the table that

00:30:26.890 --> 00:30:31.250
is really important for
how we build out things.

00:30:31.250 --> 00:30:34.840
So I often find that it's
not the most technical person

00:30:34.840 --> 00:30:36.470
in the room that
has the best ideas,

00:30:36.470 --> 00:30:40.175
it's the person with the
most direct experience

00:30:40.175 --> 00:30:42.050
with the problem that
you're trying to solve.

00:30:42.050 --> 00:30:44.980
So yeah, that's a
long winded approach

00:30:44.980 --> 00:30:48.550
to say that I absolutely
love that community

00:30:48.550 --> 00:30:52.900
and very proud to be
kind of part of it.

00:30:52.900 --> 00:30:55.000
That's wonderful.

00:30:55.000 --> 00:30:59.890
I see a lot of comments here
on community too, in the chat.

00:30:59.890 --> 00:31:02.150
And Alan, I think you just
asked a question here.

00:31:02.150 --> 00:31:03.400
I can't read it all right now.

00:31:03.400 --> 00:31:06.900
But do you want to jump in?

00:31:06.900 --> 00:31:09.580
Yeah, maybe I
wrote too much in a--

00:31:09.580 --> 00:31:11.412
Well no I just
meant I couldn't like,

00:31:11.412 --> 00:31:13.870
see it that quickly, and then
know if there was a question.

00:31:13.870 --> 00:31:15.590
But I think it's in there!

00:31:15.590 --> 00:31:18.730
Yeah there is a question
there, eventually.

00:31:18.730 --> 00:31:21.100
I think this is probably
applicable beyond Workbench,

00:31:21.100 --> 00:31:24.190
but my thinking about
it now really started

00:31:24.190 --> 00:31:25.690
when you were talking
about the sort

00:31:25.690 --> 00:31:28.540
of gradual expansion of that as
a tool to a broader audience.

00:31:28.540 --> 00:31:31.000
And what I'm experiencing
a lot right now

00:31:31.000 --> 00:31:35.230
is tension between portfolio
simplification of tools

00:31:35.230 --> 00:31:37.840
versus autonomy,
and I wonder what

00:31:37.840 --> 00:31:42.970
that conversation was like for
you and your team to consider.

00:31:42.970 --> 00:31:45.490
Like, what should the scope
of something like this be?

00:31:45.490 --> 00:31:47.110
And what are the
various trade offs

00:31:47.110 --> 00:31:49.540
of trying to make
it broadly useful,

00:31:49.540 --> 00:31:53.020
maybe being better supported but
having a steeper hill to climb,

00:31:53.020 --> 00:31:56.080
versus being autonomous but
maybe less well supported?

00:31:56.080 --> 00:31:59.390
And what those
conversations were like?

00:31:59.390 --> 00:32:00.450
Yeah.

00:32:00.450 --> 00:32:03.300
So I think--

00:32:03.300 --> 00:32:06.872
We use Looker a lot internally
as our business intelligence

00:32:06.872 --> 00:32:08.580
tool, so I feel like
that's kind of where

00:32:08.580 --> 00:32:11.670
a lot of our efforts
as an analytics team

00:32:11.670 --> 00:32:13.560
have been devoted to that.

00:32:13.560 --> 00:32:17.880
But as great as Looker
is in its many ways,

00:32:17.880 --> 00:32:20.238
there are always
questions that you're not

00:32:20.238 --> 00:32:22.780
going to be able to answer with
a business intelligence tool.

00:32:22.780 --> 00:32:26.250
So we've always had people
working in R and Python

00:32:26.250 --> 00:32:27.780
and doing their own analyses.

00:32:27.780 --> 00:32:33.540
So I think a big
part of how we sold

00:32:33.540 --> 00:32:36.300
that in terms of the
autonomous side of things

00:32:36.300 --> 00:32:39.570
is, people are
working internally,

00:32:39.570 --> 00:32:42.480
or on their local machines
and things like that,

00:32:42.480 --> 00:32:45.180
and that's obviously not
analytics best practice, so we

00:32:45.180 --> 00:32:48.630
want to move to a more
cloud-computing kind

00:32:48.630 --> 00:32:49.560
of approach.

00:32:49.560 --> 00:32:52.110
And that also, in terms
of making the business

00:32:52.110 --> 00:32:54.450
case for things like
that, that helps shore up

00:32:54.450 --> 00:32:57.600
our digital security.

00:32:57.600 --> 00:33:01.020
So, making the case that we
can have a one-stop shop that

00:33:01.020 --> 00:33:05.190
supports both R and Python
users in a secure cloud

00:33:05.190 --> 00:33:08.250
environment where they get to
use the tools that they like.

00:33:08.250 --> 00:33:11.880
You're not forcing Python
users to use RStudio.

00:33:11.880 --> 00:33:15.420
You're not forcing RStudio
users to use VS Code.

00:33:15.420 --> 00:33:18.540
Like, everyone gets their
cake and gets to eat it too.

00:33:18.540 --> 00:33:20.610
I'm terrible at these
kind of phrases.

00:33:20.610 --> 00:33:25.000
But yeah I think that was
a big, big part of that.

00:33:25.000 --> 00:33:27.720
I think it remains
to be seen, right?

00:33:27.720 --> 00:33:30.420
We kind of have a small
developer license group

00:33:30.420 --> 00:33:34.920
initially, because we need
to prove that value first.

00:33:34.920 --> 00:33:37.750
So that's kind of
a big part of that.

00:33:37.750 --> 00:33:40.380
So I think once
people start to see

00:33:40.380 --> 00:33:45.210
us producing value and
centralizing on this tool, that

00:33:45.210 --> 00:33:47.790
will help us make the case
to expand the developer

00:33:47.790 --> 00:33:50.490
community on Posit.

00:33:50.490 --> 00:33:54.540
I'm also, we're in the early
stages of developing like,

00:33:54.540 --> 00:33:56.200
there's the app
that I'm working on,

00:33:56.200 --> 00:33:58.290
but a lot of the logic
that exists in that app,

00:33:58.290 --> 00:34:01.710
we're trying to move into,
because it's pulling directly

00:34:01.710 --> 00:34:05.130
from our database, and doing
some standard analyses,

00:34:05.130 --> 00:34:07.690
we're trying to move that
into an internal package.

00:34:07.690 --> 00:34:09.420
So I think once--

00:34:09.420 --> 00:34:11.489
There's like,
aligning on the tools,

00:34:11.489 --> 00:34:15.520
but then there's also aligning
on the code and the workflows.

00:34:15.520 --> 00:34:17.790
So I think internal
package development

00:34:17.790 --> 00:34:22.650
is a great inroad to not
only proving the tools value

00:34:22.650 --> 00:34:26.400
but also aligning people
on how we work with data.

00:34:26.400 --> 00:34:30.030
Because, here I am going
to be like fangirling over

00:34:30.030 --> 00:34:32.820
Emily Ryder again, but
she just recently put out

00:34:32.820 --> 00:34:35.580
a great blog post
that really laid

00:34:35.580 --> 00:34:39.270
out the differences
between SQL, R,

00:34:39.270 --> 00:34:43.199
and both Pandas and
NumPy in Python,

00:34:43.199 --> 00:34:45.048
and how they handle
nulls differently

00:34:45.048 --> 00:34:47.590
and how this leads to completely
different results sometimes.

00:34:47.590 --> 00:34:49.710
So, I think it's
really important

00:34:49.710 --> 00:34:54.239
to align on workflows, in
addition to aligning on tools.

00:34:54.239 --> 00:34:57.060
But I think that those
tools enable the workflow

00:34:57.060 --> 00:34:59.880
centralization as well,
if that makes sense.

00:34:59.880 --> 00:35:01.050
For sure.

00:35:01.050 --> 00:35:01.952
Yeah, thanks.

00:35:01.952 --> 00:35:03.660
I really like hearing
the sort of stories

00:35:03.660 --> 00:35:05.850
in this moment of
adoption and then

00:35:05.850 --> 00:35:08.455
figuring out how to go further
and what those cases are like

00:35:08.455 --> 00:35:10.080
and what those team
processes are like.

00:35:10.080 --> 00:35:15.420
So yeah, happy you had
success and hopefully there's

00:35:15.420 --> 00:35:17.190
more in there I can learn from.

00:35:17.190 --> 00:35:17.730
Thanks!

00:35:17.730 --> 00:35:18.510
Yeah.

00:35:18.510 --> 00:35:20.310
Thank you.

00:35:20.310 --> 00:35:22.290
Thank you.

