00:38:10.880 --> 00:38:14.930
Jared, I think you had a
question earlier about GitHub

00:38:14.930 --> 00:38:16.250
actions.

00:38:16.250 --> 00:38:17.090
You want to jump in?

00:38:17.090 --> 00:38:17.780
JARED WOOLFOLK: Yeah.

00:38:17.780 --> 00:38:18.870
Can you guys hear me OK?

00:38:18.870 --> 00:38:19.430
RACHEL DEMPSEY: Yes.

00:38:19.430 --> 00:38:20.305
JARED WOOLFOLK: Yeah.

00:38:20.305 --> 00:38:21.890
Natalie, I just had
a quick question

00:38:21.890 --> 00:38:26.240
about your internal checks that
you do for your programming

00:38:26.240 --> 00:38:30.590
applications, and if you have
them in an automatic fashion?

00:38:30.590 --> 00:38:35.960
Or is this something that you
essentially from the ground up,

00:38:35.960 --> 00:38:39.260
trying to build in
checks as you go along

00:38:39.260 --> 00:38:42.050
to ensure not only
consistency but integrity

00:38:42.050 --> 00:38:45.200
of the data, so you're not
trying to put so many fires out

00:38:45.200 --> 00:38:47.000
that it becomes insurmountable?

00:38:47.000 --> 00:38:48.065
If that makes sense!

00:38:48.065 --> 00:38:50.690
Oh yeah, 100%.

00:38:50.690 --> 00:38:55.730
And I feel like I'm just like,
beating this drum of the Rhino

00:38:55.730 --> 00:39:00.740
framework, but I'm still fairly
new to continuous integration.

00:39:00.740 --> 00:39:06.050
I've done a little bit of
CI/CD stuff with Power BI

00:39:06.050 --> 00:39:09.260
before this, but this is my
first time deploying a Shiny

00:39:09.260 --> 00:39:13.460
app with continuous
integration, and I did not

00:39:13.460 --> 00:39:15.770
develop it from the ground up.

00:39:15.770 --> 00:39:18.800
I really love the Rhino
package because it forces you

00:39:18.800 --> 00:39:21.950
into some best practices,
and that's one of the things

00:39:21.950 --> 00:39:23.930
that it forces you into.

00:39:23.930 --> 00:39:26.990
Since deploying the app
with that framework,

00:39:26.990 --> 00:39:29.750
I have learned a little bit
more about GitHub actions

00:39:29.750 --> 00:39:33.860
and have modified that template.

00:39:33.860 --> 00:39:36.560
"You must best practice, Tatsu."

00:39:36.560 --> 00:39:40.640
Yeah, so like for instance,
the framework, the way

00:39:40.640 --> 00:39:45.080
the GitHub action
is set up, it runs

00:39:45.080 --> 00:39:48.530
a series of different
tests on every push.

00:39:48.530 --> 00:39:50.810
And I've realized oh
my god this is just,

00:39:50.810 --> 00:39:53.330
every time I push a
change to the app,

00:39:53.330 --> 00:39:55.970
it's running these
continuous integration tests

00:39:55.970 --> 00:39:57.450
and it's just like overwhelming.

00:39:57.450 --> 00:40:00.440
So I realized "Oh, I just need
to change the YAML header"

00:40:00.440 --> 00:40:03.650
and I can make it so that
it's only on pull request

00:40:03.650 --> 00:40:05.240
that it runs these checks.

00:40:05.240 --> 00:40:07.170
So, things like that.

00:40:07.170 --> 00:40:10.430
So, that was kind of
like, a design thing that

00:40:10.430 --> 00:40:13.340
was built into the
Rhino package that

00:40:13.340 --> 00:40:15.560
forced me to learn
something that I should

00:40:15.560 --> 00:40:17.315
have been doing the whole time.

00:40:17.315 --> 00:40:21.020
But I'm in a weird
position now, where

00:40:21.020 --> 00:40:24.290
I'm like starting to
become more recognized

00:40:24.290 --> 00:40:26.280
as like a Shiny developer.

00:40:26.280 --> 00:40:28.700
My organization people are
coming to me for advice

00:40:28.700 --> 00:40:32.420
and I always tell them, like,
"I'm giving you this advice

00:40:32.420 --> 00:40:35.990
because I've made this mistake
10 billion times before

00:40:35.990 --> 00:40:37.320
and it's painful."

00:40:37.320 --> 00:40:39.320
So when I'm telling
you like it's worth

00:40:39.320 --> 00:40:43.340
learning Shiny modules,
it's because I've

00:40:43.340 --> 00:40:47.960
built 10 billion Shiny
Frankenstein apps that quickly

00:40:47.960 --> 00:40:51.650
grow in complexity and
then are horrible to debug

00:40:51.650 --> 00:40:53.520
and all of these things.

00:40:53.520 --> 00:40:56.400
So yeah, that's a long
winded explanation,

00:40:56.400 --> 00:40:59.180
but again plugging
the Rhino framework

00:40:59.180 --> 00:41:03.560
for forcing me to learn
about GitHub actions.

00:41:03.560 --> 00:41:08.210
And then, the things that I
didn't like about the default

00:41:08.210 --> 00:41:10.850
settings, that helped me
learn about GitHub actions

00:41:10.850 --> 00:41:13.710
because then I needed to learn
how to modify those settings.

00:41:13.710 --> 00:41:17.990
So, I'm running linters,
you know, again,

00:41:17.990 --> 00:41:19.700
like setting up some deployment.

00:41:19.700 --> 00:41:21.320
I don't even fully
understand how

00:41:21.320 --> 00:41:25.580
it's testing the runs on
Linux and R and everything--

00:41:25.580 --> 00:41:28.670
or Linux, and Mac and all
of these different setups.

00:41:28.670 --> 00:41:31.880
But to the vein of
building relationships,

00:41:31.880 --> 00:41:35.510
the fact that I had
that in my application

00:41:35.510 --> 00:41:38.820
made the DataOps team
take me more seriously,

00:41:38.820 --> 00:41:41.540
and they were more willing
to support my efforts

00:41:41.540 --> 00:41:44.450
to get this tooling and to get
it built out, because they saw

00:41:44.450 --> 00:41:49.850
"Oh you're not just like ragtag
Shiny apps that can never

00:41:49.850 --> 00:41:52.970
go anywhere, you're like
building a more robust ways,

00:41:52.970 --> 00:41:55.760
and therefore I'm willing to
put in the time and effort

00:41:55.760 --> 00:41:58.340
to support you."

00:41:58.340 --> 00:42:03.590
Because why would you spend all
this time and effort docker--

00:42:03.590 --> 00:42:08.570
or not doc- but like, building
an entire infrastructure

00:42:08.570 --> 00:42:12.050
in Kubernetes if the person
that you're trying to help

00:42:12.050 --> 00:42:15.570
isn't even building something
in a way that can be maintained.

00:42:15.570 --> 00:42:20.690
So I'm a big proponent of
meet your engineers halfway

00:42:20.690 --> 00:42:22.770
and you'll have a
lot more success.

00:42:22.770 --> 00:42:26.060
So where and when
possible, really, really

00:42:26.060 --> 00:42:31.100
try and demonstrate some
robust engineering practices.

00:42:31.100 --> 00:42:32.260
Awesome.

00:42:32.260 --> 00:42:33.940
Honest I see you have
your hand raised.

00:42:33.940 --> 00:42:34.780
You want to jump in?

00:42:34.780 --> 00:42:35.710
Yes.

00:42:35.710 --> 00:42:36.940
Thank you.

00:42:36.940 --> 00:42:40.330
Natalie, so our team
has recently expanded.

00:42:40.330 --> 00:42:43.030
We're not a big team,
around three or four people

00:42:43.030 --> 00:42:48.160
that use R. Obviously
we're not advanced enough

00:42:48.160 --> 00:42:49.180
to get into the Shinies.

00:42:49.180 --> 00:42:52.960
But just broadly, do you have
any tips on the best ways

00:42:52.960 --> 00:42:55.330
to kind of establish
best practices?

00:42:55.330 --> 00:42:56.770
Again we're in our infancy.

00:42:56.770 --> 00:42:57.760
We're more like.

00:42:57.760 --> 00:43:00.280
"Do we store these R
scripts in our network drive

00:43:00.280 --> 00:43:01.840
or in a SharePoint?"

00:43:01.840 --> 00:43:05.320
Just in general, we're trying
to come up with some procedures

00:43:05.320 --> 00:43:08.770
and standards and it feels like
you've done that before, you've

00:43:08.770 --> 00:43:10.100
been there.

00:43:10.100 --> 00:43:11.310
I've been there.

00:43:11.310 --> 00:43:14.910
I am there, yeah!

00:43:14.910 --> 00:43:19.390
I would say like, again,
the anthropologist in me

00:43:19.390 --> 00:43:22.810
is coming out, but like,
focus on relationships first.

00:43:22.810 --> 00:43:24.670
Because inevitably,
like, what you

00:43:24.670 --> 00:43:27.340
define as the best
practice on first case,

00:43:27.340 --> 00:43:30.490
like, you're not going to be
doing that best practice like,

00:43:30.490 --> 00:43:31.180
six months--

00:43:31.180 --> 00:43:32.110
Not always right?

00:43:32.110 --> 00:43:34.027
Like, there are certain
things that will stay,

00:43:34.027 --> 00:43:37.840
and teaching your team
to use Git branches

00:43:37.840 --> 00:43:40.700
and merge into main
and those things

00:43:40.700 --> 00:43:41.950
are going to be pretty stable.

00:43:41.950 --> 00:43:44.320
But like, there's other
more thorny questions

00:43:44.320 --> 00:43:47.650
that are really hard to
build best practices around.

00:43:47.650 --> 00:43:51.040
So for example, in our
team, we support a lot

00:43:51.040 --> 00:43:53.890
we support a lot of
different organizations

00:43:53.890 --> 00:43:56.410
with different data
analyses, and how

00:43:56.410 --> 00:43:59.230
do we, like what size of repo?

00:43:59.230 --> 00:44:01.570
Do we put everything
into one big GitHub repo

00:44:01.570 --> 00:44:04.930
or do we make a repo for every
organization for every project?

00:44:04.930 --> 00:44:08.590
It's always in development
and it's constantly iterating,

00:44:08.590 --> 00:44:10.120
so being comfortable
with that, I

00:44:10.120 --> 00:44:12.160
think is a key
part of developing

00:44:12.160 --> 00:44:13.930
best practices,
because it's never

00:44:13.930 --> 00:44:16.780
going to be just like a set it
and forget it best practice.

00:44:16.780 --> 00:44:20.680
It's going to be
constantly in relationship

00:44:20.680 --> 00:44:25.240
and building together
those best practices.

00:44:26.710 --> 00:44:32.210
We run an R users office
hours every two weeks.

00:44:32.210 --> 00:44:34.600
And I often don't have
an agenda or anything,

00:44:34.600 --> 00:44:37.240
and it's a great way to just
first and foremost build

00:44:37.240 --> 00:44:38.080
community.

00:44:38.080 --> 00:44:39.940
But that's also
where people come

00:44:39.940 --> 00:44:42.820
to say like, "I'm really
struggling with this; like, how

00:44:42.820 --> 00:44:43.780
do you approach this?"

00:44:43.780 --> 00:44:46.660
And now we're starting to
build those best practices.

00:44:46.660 --> 00:44:49.000
And as we build out
the whole kind of Posit

00:44:49.000 --> 00:44:51.550
onboarding
infrastructure, our hope

00:44:51.550 --> 00:44:54.520
is that we end like,
internal packages.

00:44:54.520 --> 00:44:58.720
I think building
that into vignettes,

00:44:58.720 --> 00:45:00.723
like Confluence document--

00:45:00.723 --> 00:45:02.140
I haven't tried
this out yet but I

00:45:02.140 --> 00:45:03.790
did see that Quarto
is supporting

00:45:03.790 --> 00:45:06.370
like, Confluence
documentation, which

00:45:06.370 --> 00:45:08.530
is awesome because our
team uses confluence

00:45:08.530 --> 00:45:10.480
pretty significantly.

00:45:10.480 --> 00:45:14.560
But I hate anything that's
not like, programmatic.

00:45:14.560 --> 00:45:18.400
So I want to have it as a
script in R and I can automate,

00:45:18.400 --> 00:45:20.930
or like I can get in R
and alter that script,

00:45:20.930 --> 00:45:22.700
but then send those updates.

00:45:22.700 --> 00:45:25.300
So yeah, maybe not
the best answer,

00:45:25.300 --> 00:45:27.790
but just be comfortable
with iterating

00:45:27.790 --> 00:45:30.880
and make sure that you're always
communicating with each other

00:45:30.880 --> 00:45:33.790
and building strong
relationships.

00:45:33.790 --> 00:45:34.290
Yeah.

00:45:34.290 --> 00:45:35.165
That was wonderful.

