There's just such an amazing
community of R users,

00:50:39.220 --> 00:50:43.380
and that's what made me want to
continue down this whole career

00:50:43.380 --> 00:50:45.012
path.

00:50:45.012 --> 00:50:46.560
I love it.

00:50:46.560 --> 00:50:49.460
I know we talked a bit
about building a business

00:50:49.460 --> 00:50:51.500
case in the beginning,
and we were just

00:50:51.500 --> 00:50:53.060
talking about this internally.

00:50:53.060 --> 00:50:57.610
Like, is there a way that
I could make this tool

00:50:57.610 --> 00:50:58.720
or calculator for people?

00:50:58.720 --> 00:51:00.970
And I was just wondering,
in building a business case,

00:51:00.970 --> 00:51:03.700
did you actually have
to put a monetary value

00:51:03.700 --> 00:51:06.160
to what you would provide?

00:51:06.160 --> 00:51:08.500
Or like, saving money
for automations?

00:51:08.500 --> 00:51:09.130
I don't know.

00:51:09.130 --> 00:51:10.090
Just curious.

00:51:10.090 --> 00:51:11.410
Yes, for sure.

00:51:11.410 --> 00:51:12.700
And I'll answer that.

00:51:12.700 --> 00:51:14.290
I just wanted to,
someone mentioned

00:51:14.290 --> 00:51:16.510
integrating design
thinking and data science,

00:51:16.510 --> 00:51:18.850
and I just, another
like influence of mine

00:51:18.850 --> 00:51:21.490
that's like a blog
post, course, whatever

00:51:21.490 --> 00:51:23.650
person is Brian O'Neill.

00:51:23.650 --> 00:51:26.810
He does a lot of designing
human-centered data products.

00:51:26.810 --> 00:51:29.328
So I'll just plug
his work as also

00:51:29.328 --> 00:51:31.870
being a really big influence
and someone you should check out

00:51:31.870 --> 00:51:34.280
if you want to build tools
that people actually use,

00:51:34.280 --> 00:51:36.220
which I hope we all do.

00:51:36.220 --> 00:51:39.040
But yeah, as far
as putting a number

00:51:39.040 --> 00:51:42.400
on what we would be
offering, again, I

00:51:42.400 --> 00:51:45.100
have the world's best manager.

00:51:45.100 --> 00:51:48.220
I could not go on
about that any more.

00:51:48.220 --> 00:51:52.510
He's wonderful, and much more
business savvy than I am.

00:51:52.510 --> 00:51:55.060
So he helped set me
up with the team.

00:51:55.060 --> 00:52:01.078
We did kind of like
a whole, or like--

00:52:01.078 --> 00:52:03.370
Now I'm forgetting because
I'm not the business person.

00:52:03.370 --> 00:52:07.570
But we did kind of
survey people in terms

00:52:07.570 --> 00:52:10.210
of like, how much
time are you spending

00:52:10.210 --> 00:52:11.980
building a single slide deck?

00:52:11.980 --> 00:52:14.470
Like, where are all the
places that you have to go?

00:52:14.470 --> 00:52:19.540
Like on average, you spend, say,
10 hours building this slide

00:52:19.540 --> 00:52:20.830
deck.

00:52:20.830 --> 00:52:24.520
And then hey, if all
of these data points

00:52:24.520 --> 00:52:27.130
were populated
automatically for you,

00:52:27.130 --> 00:52:30.370
how much would how much
time would be remaining?

00:52:30.370 --> 00:52:31.420
So we kind of used that.

00:52:31.420 --> 00:52:32.980
And then obviously,
like, factoring

00:52:32.980 --> 00:52:35.410
in people's salaries
and all of these things,

00:52:35.410 --> 00:52:37.570
and then came up
with a rough estimate

00:52:37.570 --> 00:52:43.240
of the time-cost savings
that building out this app

00:52:43.240 --> 00:52:44.770
would have.

00:52:44.770 --> 00:52:46.990
And building-- oh
and this is kind

00:52:46.990 --> 00:52:49.090
of another good
piece of advice, is

00:52:49.090 --> 00:52:53.290
like, the app I've built
now, I don't think anyone

00:52:53.290 --> 00:52:55.330
would have been able
to see that if I just

00:52:55.330 --> 00:52:56.650
like started with that.

00:52:56.650 --> 00:53:00.100
Building MVEs or MVP--

00:53:00.100 --> 00:53:02.560
like little pieces of the pie.

00:53:02.560 --> 00:53:05.350
Like, I started off doing
parameterized reporting

00:53:05.350 --> 00:53:10.600
with R Markdown, and people
were like, "That's amazing!"

00:53:10.600 --> 00:53:14.075
But the problem with that
is that I can't quite,

00:53:14.075 --> 00:53:16.700
people aren't going to have the
kind of flexibility on the end.

00:53:16.700 --> 00:53:17.825
So I'm like, this is great.

00:53:17.825 --> 00:53:21.760
This is how we actually populate
slide decks with data insights,

00:53:21.760 --> 00:53:25.720
but if you want the flexibility
to change the branding,

00:53:25.720 --> 00:53:28.300
alter the wording, all
of this stuff like,

00:53:28.300 --> 00:53:29.740
this is our next MVP.

00:53:29.740 --> 00:53:32.500
And then you just kind of
work your way up to now

00:53:32.500 --> 00:53:34.300
I have a production
grade Shiny app

00:53:34.300 --> 00:53:36.340
that does all of this for you.

00:53:36.340 --> 00:53:38.860
But always kind of like,
building in little chunks that

00:53:38.860 --> 00:53:41.011
get you there.

00:53:41.011 --> 00:53:42.850
That's incredibly helpful.

00:53:42.850 --> 00:53:43.370
Thank you.

00:53:43.370 --> 00:53:45.450
I know that people
are doing this,

00:53:45.450 --> 00:53:48.120
but I never know like
exactly how you do it.

00:53:48.120 --> 00:53:50.490
So that's cool that
you polled everybody

00:53:50.490 --> 00:53:55.410
and ask how much time they
were spending doing something.

00:53:55.410 --> 00:53:57.870
These are the fastest
hours of the week,

00:53:57.870 --> 00:54:00.840
so I know we're coming
to the end here!

00:54:00.840 --> 00:54:03.990
But a question I recently
started asking that I really

00:54:03.990 --> 00:54:07.560
like is what's the best--

00:54:07.560 --> 00:54:08.280
sorry not best.

00:54:08.280 --> 00:54:10.530
What's a piece of
advice that comes

00:54:10.530 --> 00:54:12.810
to mind to you
that you've either

00:54:12.810 --> 00:54:16.950
given to somebody on your
team or that you've received

00:54:16.950 --> 00:54:19.020
over the course of your career?

00:54:19.020 --> 00:54:22.635
It could be the best piece, or
just whatever comes to mind.

00:54:22.635 --> 00:54:26.210
I don't think I follow
this as much as I should,

00:54:26.210 --> 00:54:29.000
because I let my enthusiasm
get the best of me.

00:54:29.000 --> 00:54:31.700
But my manager is
always giving me

00:54:31.700 --> 00:54:34.650
the advice that is
very well given,

00:54:34.650 --> 00:54:37.400
which is under-promise,
overdeliver.

00:54:37.400 --> 00:54:39.890
Like, it's classic
advice, but that

00:54:39.890 --> 00:54:42.830
is what you need to be doing.

00:54:42.830 --> 00:54:46.130
I get so excited about building
the community around stuff

00:54:46.130 --> 00:54:49.020
that I'm like, "Look guys,
this is what I did today!"

00:54:49.020 --> 00:54:51.550
And I want to grab them
and be like, "How can you

00:54:51.550 --> 00:54:52.550
use it and do all that?"

00:54:52.550 --> 00:54:57.470
But oftentimes it's better to
just put some brakes on and say

00:54:57.470 --> 00:55:00.560
like, "I'm going to make sure
I get this really ironed out."

00:55:00.560 --> 00:55:03.500
I document how I did it.

00:55:03.500 --> 00:55:07.610
Whatever you need so that it's
not just like, "Hey, watch me

00:55:07.610 --> 00:55:09.290
program this and build it up."

00:55:09.290 --> 00:55:10.250
That's fine.

00:55:10.250 --> 00:55:12.390
I do a lot of pair
programming with people.

00:55:12.390 --> 00:55:14.750
But if you can kind
of like, get something

00:55:14.750 --> 00:55:19.610
like real nice and streamlined
and professional-looking

00:55:19.610 --> 00:55:24.330
before showing everybody,
that's kind of the way to go.

00:55:24.330 --> 00:55:25.760
So under-promise, overdeliver.

00:55:25.760 --> 00:55:30.044
Best advice that I
often don't follow!

00:55:30.044 --> 00:55:32.115
RACHEL DEMPSEY: I
